  "rating": {
    "inherent": "critical",
    "residual": "critical",
    "forecast": "critical",
    "rationale": "The dominant risk is a demonstrated MCP tool-poisoning path (f.mcp_tool_poisoning_credential_exfil) in which an untrusted, unpinned third-party tool description instructs the agent to read local AWS credentials and send them to the tool author's hosted service. No declared control severs, meaningfully narrows, or gates this path: the filesystem scope still includes the credentials folder (constrains_sink: false) and the only approval mechanism is remembered after first use, so it earns no credit. Residual therefore equals the critical inherent floor. Three further property gaps compound the exposure but are each capped at 'high' under the property-finding rule: an unscoped Slack posting capability reaching external-organization channels, no attributable audit trail for any tool action, and no step/rate/spend limits on autonomous tool use. The forecast does not rise further only because no effective control currently exists to decay; every named trigger (model_upgrade, new_mcp_server, autonomy_increase, data_scope_expansion, control_removal) should still prompt reassessment once the recommended controls are implemented."
  },
  "limitations": [
    "This assessment reflects the declared design and configuration in the provided artifacts, not observed runtime behavior.",
    "No system prompt was provided, so instruction-level issues (ambiguous goals, conflicting directives) could not be reviewed; confidence on behavioral controls is reduced accordingly.",
    "Transport security (TLS/mTLS) for the quicknotes_mcp and Slack connections is not described in the artifacts and was not assessed.",
    "The actual content of the desktop client's approval dialog is not documented; approval_review_mode was inferred as model_summary rather than verified."
  ]
